I mean, first and foremost,

16
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:47,680
people in the field that
lots of ideas recirculate.

17
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:53,380
And so, in a way, I put
together this slide deck,

18
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:56,040
or I recovered the slide deck,
from the AlphaStar project,

19
00:00:56,040 --> 00:01:00,340
which is six or so
years old, and realized

20
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:06,500
that many of the current issues
but also solutions that we're

21
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:13,080
using in modern LLMs
trace back further before.

22
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:17,540
But I mean, we did encounter
many of these issues

23
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:20,580
ourselves when we work
on a fairly, probably

24
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:25,220
our most advanced, even today,
especially on video games, agent

25
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:27,360
that DeepMind had
built at the time.

26
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,420
And I'll get into
details now in a second.

27
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:34,890
But it's useful to revisit
these ideas to see them

28
00:01:34,890 --> 00:01:36,850
with perspective
and also realize

29
00:01:36,850 --> 00:01:41,130
what are the things that we did
back then that, currently, we

30
00:01:41,130 --> 00:01:45,650
can't do, or we're not quite
doing at the same level in LLM.

31
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:49,650
So this lecture will be a bit
of a mixture between AlphaStar

32
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:54,130
and also Gemini and
other LLM agents

33
00:01:54,130 --> 00:01:58,370
but with the kind of hindsight,
foresight lens, which hopefully,

34
00:01:58,370 --> 00:02:00,890
you will find entertaining.

35
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:03,410
So let's go through
a bit of basics.

36
00:02:03,410 --> 00:02:06,470
Now of course, I'm sure you've
covered many of these topics.

37
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:08,130
And you probably know
them because they

38
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:09,670
become quite mainstream.

39
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:13,410
But indulge me into giving
you my own perspective

40
00:02:13,410 --> 00:02:15,410
and point of view.

41
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:20,130
So this is the OG slide
of DeepMind, actually.

42
00:02:20,130 --> 00:02:22,650
The pitch slide
deck had a version

43
00:02:22,650 --> 00:02:24,310
of this that did
not have StarCraft,

44
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:27,890
but it had agent environment,
agent with goals, observations,

45
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:28,550
and actions.

46
00:02:28,550 --> 00:02:31,550
That was, at the time,
not that mainstream.

47
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:34,510
Reinforcement learning
wasn't mainstream

48
00:02:34,510 --> 00:02:37,550
when DeepMind was
eventually acquired.

49
00:02:37,550 --> 00:02:40,190
And also, it serves
a little bit to try

50
00:02:40,190 --> 00:02:43,710
to put our heads on what is
an agent since this class is

51
00:02:43,710 --> 00:02:44,830
about agents.

52
00:02:44,830 --> 00:02:50,530
And I think today if you ask
people, like, is this an agent,

53
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:52,750
is this an agentic
behavior, you probably

54
00:02:52,750 --> 00:02:55,509
get quite a few
different answers.

55
00:02:55,509 --> 00:02:58,790
Back then, things were
a bit simpler, though.

56
00:02:58,790 --> 00:03:01,910
We were mostly connecting
to single environments

57
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:05,150
with, well, [? spect ?]
and defined observations

58
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:06,010
and actions.

59
00:03:06,010 --> 00:03:10,550
And the agent had just,
maximize reward as a goal.

60
00:03:10,550 --> 00:03:13,390
Of course, in games,
many times, the reward

61
00:03:13,390 --> 00:03:16,830
was fully specified and
fairly simple to compute.

62
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:21,010
And then this was the loop
that agents lived in forever.

63
00:03:21,010 --> 00:03:23,710
And their goal was
to optimize reward.

64
00:03:23,710 --> 00:03:29,940
Now today, things
are a bit different.

65
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:33,960
Besides, I guess, having agents
in the traditional sense,

66
00:03:33,960 --> 00:03:36,700
we have assistants
that assist users.

67
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:39,980
So then, what is the
environment, what

68
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:42,840
are the actions, start
to be a bit more diffuse.

69
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:45,860
And I think embracing
that is fine.

70
00:03:45,860 --> 00:03:48,900
This is probably
the most accurate,

71
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:51,740
at least from our
side, perspective

72
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,920
of what we mean by agents.

73
00:03:53,920 --> 00:04:00,820
So you might be a user of a
chatbot or advanced system.

74
00:04:00,820 --> 00:04:03,780
And you're going to interact
with something, this object.

75
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:05,820
We can call this the agent.

76
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:10,800
And as a user, you'll ask
questions, maybe give feedback,

77
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:12,000
et cetera, et cetera.

78
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,060
And then this agent now
actually is more complex.

79
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:17,420
In a new way, I think
it's more useful to think

80
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:21,800
about the environment being
part of the whole package.

81
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:25,420
So you'll get an
agent harness that's

82
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:30,880
controlling how you call
the LLMs to issue an action

83
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:34,640
or issue a search or issue a
terminal command, et cetera,

84
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:36,160
to this very rich
environment that

85
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:40,545
could be a digital computer,
a network of computers.

86
00:04:40,545 --> 00:04:41,420
It could be anything.

87
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:44,680
It could be a game, of
course, as well, very general.

88
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:47,980
And then that's how we
orchestrate the whole thing.

89
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:53,240
So it doesn't quite fit
the paradigm as neatly.

90
00:04:53,240 --> 00:04:56,920
But that actually things--
makes it more exciting.

91
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:59,880
So sometimes when I'm
presenting some of the results

92
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:02,740
and observations,
especially from AlphaStar,

93
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:06,200
it's going to come from a
more simplistic world, where

94
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:10,120
single environment, clearly
defined, and no user, so

95
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:10,660
to speak.

96
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:11,900
So the world has changed.

97
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,760
And these will keep
evolving, especially

98
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:16,658
as we get to an exciting
topic that, I think,

99
00:05:16,658 --> 00:05:18,200
is the one I want
to leave you, maybe

100
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:21,080
with some
thought-provoking ideas

101
00:05:21,080 --> 00:05:23,620
and observations on
multi-agent systems,

102
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:25,460
and what does that
even mean here.

103
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:28,950
So a bit of history.

104
00:05:28,950 --> 00:05:31,550
The DeepMind, at the
time of acquisition,

105
00:05:31,550 --> 00:05:33,490
had just mastered Atari.

106
00:05:33,490 --> 00:05:36,950
I remember fondly
the NeurIPS in which

107
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:38,970
Vlad presented the results.

108
00:05:38,970 --> 00:05:41,230
And they felt really refreshing.

109
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:44,810
I believe that was the
sequence-to-sequence in NeurIPS.

110
00:05:44,810 --> 00:05:46,270
Maybe it was the year before.

111
00:05:46,270 --> 00:05:47,450
I can't remember.

112
00:05:47,450 --> 00:05:48,950
It was a while ago.

113
00:05:48,950 --> 00:05:52,330
And then in the internal
program for DeepMind,

114
00:05:52,330 --> 00:05:55,270
there was sort of a curriculum
of difficulty of games

115
00:05:55,270 --> 00:05:57,470
that we were going to tackle.

116
00:05:57,470 --> 00:06:02,770
And famously, Go came a
few years after Atari.

117
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:05,110
And then I would
say, maybe StarCraft

118
00:06:05,110 --> 00:06:09,170
graduated as to just master
all due to its complexity.

119
00:06:09,170 --> 00:06:11,590
Although, of course, there's
many interesting games

120
00:06:11,590 --> 00:06:13,690
that have slightly
different complexities.

121
00:06:13,690 --> 00:06:18,510
But StarCraft, clearly for
AI, felt like a good kind

122
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:19,450
of ending note.

123
00:06:19,450 --> 00:06:22,850
Although even today, we
are working on games still,

124
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:26,930
as you might have seen with
things like WOT models.

125
00:06:26,930 --> 00:06:30,730
And SEMA 2.0 was just released
that can play 3D games

126
00:06:30,730 --> 00:06:31,230
and so on.

127
00:06:31,230 --> 00:06:34,210
But the approach
is quite different.

128
00:06:34,210 --> 00:06:36,510
Now, blast from
the past as well.

129
00:06:36,510 --> 00:06:40,770
So 2011, ages ago, I used
to be a grad student.

130
00:06:40,770 --> 00:06:44,290
And very exciting
from Dan Kline's group

131
00:06:44,290 --> 00:06:49,890
was this project that tried
to tackle solving StarCraft

132
00:06:49,890 --> 00:06:51,150
with machine learning.

133
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:54,590
And there was a whole
class set for testing,

134
00:06:54,590 --> 00:06:59,010
kind of maybe somewhat
good, old-style-fashion AI,

135
00:06:59,010 --> 00:07:02,330
although some machine
learning as well,

136
00:07:02,330 --> 00:07:04,730
interleave with just
trying to play the game

137
00:07:04,730 --> 00:07:07,930
or play different
aspects of the game.

138
00:07:07,930 --> 00:07:10,190
So it was recognized,
of course, as a domain.

139
00:07:10,190 --> 00:07:12,810
And personally, of course,
this resonated very much

140
00:07:12,810 --> 00:07:15,130
when I saw DeepMind for
the first time applying

141
00:07:15,130 --> 00:07:17,750
ML, which I love, to video
games, which I love--

142
00:07:17,750 --> 00:07:19,610
I used to play StarCraft--

143
00:07:19,610 --> 00:07:24,920
to eventually become the lead
of the AlphaStar project.

144
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:27,340
So very, very interesting
domain at the time.

145
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:30,840
In 2011, really, it
seemed quite impossible

146
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:33,240
that a single neural
net could play the game.

147
00:07:33,240 --> 00:07:36,000
But that's basically
what we did.

148
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,500
And for those of you who
do not know StarCraft,

149
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:42,160
I mean, it's very hard to give
you a two-minute overview.

150
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:45,640
But it's essentially a
chess in which you don't

151
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:47,020
see the board all the time.

152
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:50,520
And things are fairly
continuous, like actions.

153
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:53,440
It's a real-time strategy game.

154
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:56,480
So there's lots of hiding
information from the enemy

155
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:58,800
because it's
partial, observable.

156
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:01,640
And generally, the action
space is quite complex

157
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:04,240
because you have to control
not only the strategy

158
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:06,880
at the high level but
also the microstrategy

159
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:09,580
about how to move your
units about the map.

160
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:11,260
So here you have a map.

161
00:08:11,260 --> 00:08:12,720
It has resources.

162
00:08:12,720 --> 00:08:14,180
You gather resources.

163
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:14,900
You get money.

164
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:15,860
You build buildings.

165
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:16,780
You build units.

166
00:08:16,780 --> 00:08:18,480
You engage the enemy.

167
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:20,460
And if you destroy all
the enemy buildings,

168
00:08:20,460 --> 00:08:22,860
that's the winning
or losing condition.

169
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:25,060
So it's kind of a fun game.

170
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:28,400
It's been quite heavily
played over the years,

171
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:29,480
different versions of it.

172
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:34,200
And it brought esports to life
in South Korea, certainly very,

173
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:37,400
very early days, in
the late 1990s even.

174
00:08:37,400 --> 00:08:40,900
So this kind of
pioneered, in a way also,

175
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:43,620
certain aspects of video gaming.

176
00:08:43,620 --> 00:08:45,740
Different points of
view, this is the one

177
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:49,380
you might have actually seen
that's rendered directly

178
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:50,120
from the game.

179
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:52,560
This is your UI.

180
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:56,060
you only see on the bottom
left here, part of the map.

181
00:08:56,060 --> 00:08:59,180
You need to move the camera,
send the units around, build

182
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:01,300
buildings, issue commands.

183
00:09:01,300 --> 00:09:02,980
It's fairly fast-paced.

184
00:09:02,980 --> 00:09:06,060
So there's lots of
cool subproblems

185
00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:09,500
that you could tackle
as an AI domain.

186
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:12,020
But as I was saying, what
we actually end up doing--

187
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:14,860
and I'll describe bits and
pieces but not in full--

188
00:09:14,860 --> 00:09:16,820
we trained a single
neural network, basically,

189
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:19,380
that was issuing all
these commands and how

190
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:23,390
and it matters to LLM or current
trends that we're seeing.

191
00:09:23,390 --> 00:09:25,870
I'll describe in the
second part of the talk.

192
00:09:25,870 --> 00:09:29,290
And then in the third part,
we'll cover multi-agent.

193
00:09:29,290 --> 00:09:32,950
So achievement wise,
I mean, it was fun.

194
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:36,130
And I remember
fondly when our agent

195
00:09:36,130 --> 00:09:38,010
achieved grandmaster
status, which

196
00:09:38,010 --> 00:09:43,090
is given only to the top 100
players in any given region.

197
00:09:43,090 --> 00:09:45,310
So we were playing online,
literally in our office.

198
00:09:45,310 --> 00:09:47,690
So imagine playing
video games at work.

199
00:09:47,690 --> 00:09:49,170
That was pretty fun.

200
00:09:49,170 --> 00:09:52,770
And we were all sitting around,
playing games against people

201
00:09:52,770 --> 00:09:55,670
that didn't know they were
playing against AlphaStar,

202
00:09:55,670 --> 00:09:57,270
although many could guess.

203
00:09:57,270 --> 00:09:59,290
And there was lots
of speculation

204
00:09:59,290 --> 00:10:02,690
because you had to opt in to
play against possibly advanced

205
00:10:02,690 --> 00:10:03,330
AIs.

206
00:10:03,330 --> 00:10:05,970
But eventually, we reached
the grandmaster milestone.

207
00:10:05,970 --> 00:10:09,250
And then later, we also
went sort of in the wild

208
00:10:09,250 --> 00:10:13,090
in BlizzCon, which is a NeurIPS
for StarCraft, so to speak,

209
00:10:13,090 --> 00:10:15,810
where we played
many, many folks who

210
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:19,250
just were mesmerized by playing
against an AI at the time.

211
00:10:19,250 --> 00:10:22,190
This was, I believe,
our first AI system

212
00:10:22,190 --> 00:10:25,310
in the wild from
DeepMind, actually.

213
00:10:25,310 --> 00:10:28,250
So it was quite nice to
just interact with people,

214
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:30,730
see how they felt about
the game, AI, et cetera.

215
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:34,350
This was 2019, right
before the pandemic hit.

216
00:10:34,350 --> 00:10:38,350
And so we visited this
wonderful, very different style,

217
00:10:38,350 --> 00:10:40,010
conference, I would
say than NeurIPS,

218
00:10:40,010 --> 00:10:43,070
although I think NeurIPS is
becoming a bit like this as well

219
00:10:43,070 --> 00:10:44,630
lately.

220
00:10:44,630 --> 00:10:45,610
And it was fun.

221
00:10:45,610 --> 00:10:48,470
We played a bunch
of people, including

222
00:10:48,470 --> 00:10:50,850
Serral, who was one of
the legends of the game.

223
00:10:50,850 --> 00:10:55,470
If you want to see the games,
the five games we played,

224
00:10:55,470 --> 00:10:58,530
I believe YouTube links
are here at your disposal.

225
00:10:58,530 --> 00:11:01,310
You can see here,
me, a bit tense

226
00:11:01,310 --> 00:11:05,570
