227
00:11:05,570 --> 00:11:06,750
But it was all fun.

228
00:11:06,750 --> 00:11:09,990
And I think probably the
most profound realization

229
00:11:09,990 --> 00:11:14,190
was a couple of
weeks after, you see,

230
00:11:14,190 --> 00:11:18,340
November 24, Serral
mentioned that he actually

231
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:21,540
copied StarCraft or AlphaStar,
one of the [INAUDIBLE]

232
00:11:21,540 --> 00:11:23,540
that he had played against.

233
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:29,100
And of course, an obvious
comment that, again, in a way,

234
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:33,620
concluded this epoch we had
at DeepMind on mastering games

235
00:11:33,620 --> 00:11:38,820
in one-on-one setting, was
that we got a player learning

236
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:39,760
from AlphaStar.

237
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:42,240
But of course, AlphaStar
had learned from several,

238
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:43,800
as you will see in a second.

239
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:46,140
So that was pretty fun.

240
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:49,900
So let's start to see, maybe
some obvious, some less obvious,

241
00:11:49,900 --> 00:11:51,780
connections.

242
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:57,920
And then yeah, basically,
why does it all make sense?

243
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:02,820
Even when we talk to reporters
at the time, very early days,

244
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:04,780
they didn't
understand the impact

245
00:12:04,780 --> 00:12:07,706
of playing games in real world.

246
00:12:07,706 --> 00:12:11,685
So here I try to map,
fairly imperfectly,

247
00:12:11,685 --> 00:12:16,000
the timeline of certain major
projects, Alpha asterisk

248
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:20,360
projects, if you will,
that kind of, in a way,

249
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:24,480
took inspiration, either
algorithmically or learnings

250
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:28,440
or whatnot, from the
previous projects that

251
00:12:28,440 --> 00:12:33,560
had come in because games
were such a good sandbox

252
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:37,240
to try some ideas to
scale up neural networks.

253
00:12:37,240 --> 00:12:40,240
And I mean, at the time, it
was a bit hard to convey this

254
00:12:40,240 --> 00:12:42,560
to, maybe, general public.

255
00:12:42,560 --> 00:12:46,600
But it's great to see that
this kind of plan for, really,

256
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:50,240
the whole company was set
about, is taking shape.

257
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:52,860
And games still have a
role to play, by the way,

258
00:12:52,860 --> 00:12:57,440
into building, pursuing
AGI in the context of LLMs.

259
00:12:57,440 --> 00:13:00,140
But here, there's
a few projects.

260
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:01,300
I didn't put all of them.

261
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:03,960
Certainly, there's no
Atari, for example.

262
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:06,060
But certainly, as I
mentioned, after Go,

263
00:13:06,060 --> 00:13:07,920
we decided to tackle StarCraft.

264
00:13:07,920 --> 00:13:09,340
And then there's no other games.

265
00:13:09,340 --> 00:13:12,240
But after that, we
had huge inspiration

266
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:16,630
to start working on coding
early days on our first LLMs--

267
00:13:16,630 --> 00:13:17,570
Chinchilla.

268
00:13:17,570 --> 00:13:21,790
Visual language models
Flamingo or Gato.

269
00:13:21,790 --> 00:13:24,650
And AlphaFold also took
inspiration of, I mean,

270
00:13:24,650 --> 00:13:28,310
certain aspects of transformers,
distillation of data, et cetera.

271
00:13:28,310 --> 00:13:31,790
And today, as you
probably can guess,

272
00:13:31,790 --> 00:13:37,030
a lot of the research and
resources are now heavily

273
00:13:37,030 --> 00:13:39,378
invested into a single
project, almost,

274
00:13:39,378 --> 00:13:41,170
although it's very
large-- so there's many,

275
00:13:41,170 --> 00:13:42,910
many different components--

276
00:13:42,910 --> 00:13:46,070
called Gemini, which I'm
co-tech leading, together

277
00:13:46,070 --> 00:13:47,750
with Jeff and Noah.

278
00:13:47,750 --> 00:13:49,630
And of course, in
there, we're just

279
00:13:49,630 --> 00:13:52,690
trying to build the most
general system we can.

280
00:13:52,690 --> 00:13:55,430
So a game or playing
a single game

281
00:13:55,430 --> 00:13:58,990
might be one of the many, many
things, the incredible things

282
00:13:58,990 --> 00:14:01,150
that these models can do today.

283
00:14:01,150 --> 00:14:04,630
But it's nice to also look back
and attribute a bit of ideas

284
00:14:04,630 --> 00:14:07,270
or where things come from.

285
00:14:07,270 --> 00:14:12,540
So more specifically, let's dive
into some technical details.

286
00:14:12,540 --> 00:14:16,160
And some have striking
closeness, I would say,

287
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:20,780
with how today we're creating
some of these amazing LLMs.

288
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:24,740
So this is from the Nature paper
that was eventually published.

289
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:27,280
And we'll discuss
the three aspects.

290
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:30,780
But in sequence,
we did, basically,

291
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:33,480
three things to create
this single neural network.

292
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:35,980
We had, first,
supervised learning,

293
00:14:35,980 --> 00:14:40,360
as we called it in this figure,
learning from human data.

294
00:14:40,360 --> 00:14:43,420
Then we had a step of
reinforcement learning.

295
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:46,120
And this reinforcement learning
didn't happen in a vacuum.

296
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:48,620
It happened in a massive
multi-agent system,

297
00:14:48,620 --> 00:14:51,780
like as follows.

298
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:57,260
So mapping to today's
projects or work,

299
00:14:57,260 --> 00:14:59,840
this is what we commonly
now call pre-training.

300
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:01,500
This is post-training.

301
00:15:01,500 --> 00:15:03,393
And I don't think
there's a massive amount

302
00:15:03,393 --> 00:15:05,060
of [? motivation ?]
work, although there

303
00:15:05,060 --> 00:15:08,500
is more and more, of course,
as we get more mature

304
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:11,240
in the technology for LLMs.

305
00:15:11,240 --> 00:15:14,320
But even at the
highest level of how

306
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:17,040
the different steps of
creating this agent,

307
00:15:17,040 --> 00:15:19,120
we already could
see, or we already

308
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:22,560
can see that many
of the problems

309
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:26,980
we faced back then apply today.

310
00:15:26,980 --> 00:15:29,120
And you will see why
in more detail now

311
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:32,040
for each of these stages.

312
00:15:32,040 --> 00:15:36,040
So let's start with actually
something that at the time

313
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:39,640
felt kind of complicated
and also, in a way,

314
00:15:39,640 --> 00:15:43,000
obvious in hindsight, having
worked on sequence models, which

315
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,559
is, how to deal with
actions, the actions

316
00:15:45,559 --> 00:15:48,040
that you needed to
issue to activate

317
00:15:48,040 --> 00:15:51,200
or to operate StarCraft.

318
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:53,900
So the actions were fixed.

319
00:15:53,900 --> 00:15:55,200
There was an API.

320
00:15:55,200 --> 00:16:00,520
And as an API, you basically had
all sorts of different functions

321
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:01,359
that you could call.

322
00:16:01,359 --> 00:16:05,000
And different functions
required different arguments.

323
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,150
So this is basically
an API that Blizzard

324
00:16:08,150 --> 00:16:11,350
has and had before we started
working with them already

325
00:16:11,350 --> 00:16:15,030
to interact or to send
network messages, actually,

326
00:16:15,030 --> 00:16:16,870
between players in StarCraft.

327
00:16:16,870 --> 00:16:19,630
And we just used it as is.

328
00:16:19,630 --> 00:16:22,130
So you could imagine
in this instance,

329
00:16:22,130 --> 00:16:24,670
there's this thing here
that blocks this ramp.

330
00:16:24,670 --> 00:16:26,770
So there's an action
called forcefield,

331
00:16:26,770 --> 00:16:28,950
which is the name of this thing.

332
00:16:28,950 --> 00:16:32,770
And you issue this action
to this set of units.

333
00:16:32,770 --> 00:16:34,950
So you're saying,
hey, set of units,

334
00:16:34,950 --> 00:16:41,030
try to issue a forcefield
in these coordinates, 6, 82.

335
00:16:41,030 --> 00:16:44,830
And then you also say,
delay from current.

336
00:16:44,830 --> 00:16:48,270
You can wait 10 steps, which
is kind of half a second

337
00:16:48,270 --> 00:16:49,310
or something.

338
00:16:49,310 --> 00:16:51,770
And only repeat
this action once.

339
00:16:51,770 --> 00:16:55,990
So that's how you do
everything in the game.

340
00:16:55,990 --> 00:16:59,230
And it was a bit tricky
to how would we be

341
00:16:59,230 --> 00:17:01,350
able to issue these commands.

342
00:17:01,350 --> 00:17:06,430
So the solution we
found is to just lay out

343
00:17:06,430 --> 00:17:09,570
an autoregressive model, a kind
of a little recurrent neural

344
00:17:09,570 --> 00:17:13,610
network that was structured so
that all these arguments were

345
00:17:13,610 --> 00:17:17,010
lining up with different
softmaxes, that would basically

346
00:17:17,010 --> 00:17:19,670
say, Q, yes or no, when.

347
00:17:19,670 --> 00:17:23,090
And then it's a softmax
between 0 and 128 steps

348
00:17:23,090 --> 00:17:25,690
or whatever maximum
we decided, what

349
00:17:25,690 --> 00:17:30,370
is also softmax, a categorical
softmax, over positions,

350
00:17:30,370 --> 00:17:31,270
et cetera, et cetera.

351
00:17:31,270 --> 00:17:34,810
So we handcrafted
this architecture.

352
00:17:34,810 --> 00:17:39,570
And then we obviously learned
both imitation and reinforcement

353
00:17:39,570 --> 00:17:44,890
learning to finesse how to issue
these sequence of instructions

354
00:17:44,890 --> 00:17:48,690
or commands that form
an API call that then we

355
00:17:48,690 --> 00:17:50,330
would send to the game.

356
00:17:50,330 --> 00:17:54,830
And this is strikingly close
to tool use in general.

357
00:17:54,830 --> 00:17:57,690
Because in tool use
in LLMs, what we

358
00:17:57,690 --> 00:17:59,650
have is an access to an API.

359
00:17:59,650 --> 00:18:00,290
There's MCP.

360
00:18:00,290 --> 00:18:01,790
There's so many
different protocols.

361
00:18:01,790 --> 00:18:03,950
But essentially, there's an API.

362
00:18:03,950 --> 00:18:08,600
There's a way to define
how to call a search engine

363
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:10,980
or issue a command
on a terminal,

364
00:18:10,980 --> 00:18:14,400
or click on the screen for
that matter, all these things.

365
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:18,100
And then this is defined
now in natural language.

366
00:18:18,100 --> 00:18:23,240
But actually, lots of different
problems and solutions,

367
00:18:23,240 --> 00:18:24,420
again, align here.

368
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:26,052
And it was cool too.

369
00:18:26,052 --> 00:18:28,260
At the time, I mentioned
when I talk about AlphaStar,

370
00:18:28,260 --> 00:18:29,760
AlphaStar is like a language.

371
00:18:29,760 --> 00:18:32,960
You just need to issue
these, literally,

372
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:36,480
code calls or API
calls into the game.

373
00:18:36,480 --> 00:18:41,520
And there are some differences
that are notable as well.

374
00:18:41,520 --> 00:18:46,760
Here, this architecture
is setting the API.

375
00:18:46,760 --> 00:18:48,960
So the aggressive
actions are expressed

376
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:51,520
through this particular
architecture.

377
00:18:51,520 --> 00:18:54,040
As cool as it is, it's fixed.

378
00:18:54,040 --> 00:18:58,760
Whereas today, we know that
to issue API calls using

379
00:18:58,760 --> 00:19:03,140
the power of generalization of
text or code or combinations

380
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:07,300
allows us to issue
API calls to all sorts

381
00:19:07,300 --> 00:19:10,980
of different environments that
agents might be connected to.

382
00:19:10,980 --> 00:19:13,460
We also have now a single
transformer architecture

383
00:19:13,460 --> 00:19:17,020
that just writes these
text or code for us that

384
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:20,660
then is interpreted and
sent to the environment.

385
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:23,980
And then there's actually
a lot of flexibility even

386
00:19:23,980 --> 00:19:26,820
in the formats because you can
instruct the models to change

387
00:19:26,820 --> 00:19:29,540
the formats if you want to be
empirical about which formats

388
00:19:29,540 --> 00:19:32,420
might be best, versus
here, where there's

389
00:19:32,420 --> 00:19:34,560
a certain architectural
rigidity then.

390
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:38,900
So of course, we did
ablate many decisions.

391
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:41,180
And we had to retrain the
models and then try which

392
00:19:41,180 --> 00:19:43,060
ones play the game better.

393
00:19:43,060 --> 00:19:49,180
But it was much more cumbersome
if we need to retrain.

394
00:19:49,180 --> 00:19:51,820
Now another very
big problem, and I

395
00:19:51,820 --> 00:19:54,700
think this is actually a
key debate, even today.

396
00:19:54,700 --> 00:19:58,500
It's still pretty hot
between many folks, which

397
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:03,170
are, the issue of using
imitation learning, which

398
00:20:03,170 --> 00:20:07,490
we commonly denote as
pre-training in the LLM world.

399
00:20:07,490 --> 00:20:12,370
So in StarCraft, unlike,
let's say, Go or Atari,

400
00:20:12,370 --> 00:20:14,830
where the action
space, however large,

401
00:20:14,830 --> 00:20:18,970
was literally not as large
as it is in StarCraft,

402
00:20:18,970 --> 00:20:23,170
where it's very combinatorial,
and also the time horizon

403
00:20:23,170 --> 00:20:25,490
was much longer, et
cetera, et cetera,

404
00:20:25,490 --> 00:20:28,470
exploration is and
was very challenging.

405
00:20:28,470 --> 00:20:31,970
And I'll demonstrate that
kind of pretty sharply

406
00:20:31,970 --> 00:20:34,370
in the next video.

407
00:20:34,370 --> 00:20:38,370
So the problem is, random
policy will not do much

408
00:20:38,370 --> 00:20:40,810
in this game that's useful.

409
00:20:40,810 --> 00:20:44,210
And so in a way, the
bitter lesson discussion,

410
00:20:44,210 --> 00:20:49,090
which is, should you gather
as much imitation learning

411
00:20:49,090 --> 00:20:52,650
experience to bootstrap the
knowledge about the domain

412
00:20:52,650 --> 00:20:55,290
even in a game like
StarCraft, which arguably,

413
00:20:55,290 --> 00:21:00,070
is not AGI complete,
as complex as it is,

414
00:21:00,070 --> 00:21:02,670
didn't work to the
best of our abilities.

415
00:21:02,670 --> 00:21:06,950
So for us, it was very clear
we would need an imitation

416
00:21:06,950 --> 00:21:11,110
learning plus then an experience
learning, reinforcement

417
00:21:11,110 --> 00:21:13,710
learning, loop on top.

418
00:21:13,710 --> 00:21:18,590
And that was kind of a nice
merging of deep learning

419
00:21:18,590 --> 00:21:22,190
and reinforcement learning,
which I very much welcome also

420
00:21:22,190 --> 00:21:28,510
in LLMs, although it can be
debated whether, ultimately,

421
00:21:28,510 --> 00:21:30,450
pure reinforcement
learning, for example,

422
00:21:30,450 --> 00:21:33,170
could get us all the way there.

423
00:21:33,170 --> 00:21:34,810
I strongly believe
that answer is no,

424
00:21:34,810 --> 00:21:37,390
as you can probably guess.

425
00:21:37,390 --> 00:21:41,630
So what happens when you apply
pure reinforcement learning is

426
00:21:41,630 --> 00:21:44,150
that it actually
discovers how to beat

427
00:21:44,150 --> 00:21:46,770
the enemy but in a way
that is unsatisfactory.

428
00:21:46,770 --> 00:21:49,030
It takes all your workers.

429
00:21:49,030 --> 00:21:51,250
And then it just
sends them to attack.

430
00:21:51,250 --> 00:21:53,610
This is not an
uncommon technique.

431
00:21:53,610 --> 00:21:57,170
I mean, if you're a
newbie, you might do it.

432
00:21:57,170 --> 00:22:00,600
Some professional gamers do it
at some point with [? aliens, ?]

433
00:22:00,600 --> 00:22:03,820
especially that they also bring
all the workers to attack.

434
00:22:03,820 --> 00:22:07,540
This one beat the hardest AI
in the game by exploiting it,

435
00:22:07,540 --> 00:22:09,420
basically with
something that was not

436
00:22:09,420 --> 00:22:11,180
pre-programmed to
defend very well

437
00:22:11,180 --> 00:22:13,580
against this kind of attack.

438
00:22:13,580 --> 00:22:17,660
But clearly, we just kept
seeing these results.

439
00:22:17,660 --> 00:22:19,860
And we had to resort
to essentially using

440
00:22:19,860 --> 00:22:22,600
human data, which
luckily, we did.

441
00:22:22,600 --> 00:22:25,660
We open-sourced over 1
million games of StarCraft,

442
00:22:25,660 --> 00:22:29,780
thanks to Blizzard collecting
anonymous replays online.

443
00:22:29,780 --> 00:22:31,600
They're still available
today, hopefully.

444
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:33,860
And there's a good
cool way to do research

445
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:36,780
on imitation learning.

446
00:22:36,780 --> 00:22:41,620
But basically, getting
lots of human data online

447
00:22:41,620 --> 00:22:46,380
was felt necessary and
was part of the solution.

448
00:22:46,380 --> 00:22:50,100
And then something else we
did which also, at the time,

449
00:22:50,100 --> 00:22:51,520
felt very different.

450
00:22:51,520 --> 00:22:55,220
But besides saying, hey,
imitate every human move

451
00:22:55,220 --> 00:22:58,620
from these millions of
games, we also said,

452
00:22:58,620 --> 00:23:01,860
look, hold on a second,
from the whole game,

453
00:23:01,860 --> 00:23:03,220
we know what happened.

454
00:23:03,220 --> 00:23:05,620
We know how good
the players were.

455
00:23:05,620 --> 00:23:07,100
We have some metadata.

456
00:23:07,100 --> 00:23:09,420
We know what the players
eventually built,

457
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:11,400
what units they did.

458
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:15,100
So these metadata, we
decided to encode it.

459
00:23:15,100 --> 00:23:16,600
It's a bit of a
cheating information

460
00:23:16,600 --> 00:23:18,058
because, as you
start the game, you

461
00:23:18,058 --> 00:23:22,640
don't know the future units
that will appear, et cetera.

462
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:26,600
But it was sort of
a guiding signal

463
00:23:26,600 --> 00:23:30,000
to condition the
neural network to look

464
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:31,580
at these for correlations.

465
00:23:31,580 --> 00:23:35,080
So for instance, the neural
net would, OK, I need to do--

466
00:23:35,080 --> 00:23:36,502
this kind of unit will appear.

467
00:23:36,502 --> 00:23:38,960
It would learn the correlation
between these units and then

468
00:23:38,960 --> 00:23:41,820
how to build it even in
imitation learning setting,

469
00:23:41,820 --> 00:23:44,480
even though the correlations
are fairly weak.

470
00:23:44,480 --> 00:23:47,560
And so that was kind
of a powerful thing

471
00:23:47,560 --> 00:23:49,180
to do for many reasons.

472
00:23:49,180 --> 00:23:52,680
And I'll go get back to these
conditioning signals again

473
00:23:52,680 --> 00:23:55,310
as well during the lecture.

474
00:23:55,310 --> 00:23:58,190
But again, contrasting
this with current LLMs,

475
00:23:58,190 --> 00:24:02,870
this actually was a form of
prompt or instruction following.

476
00:24:02,870 --> 00:24:08,270
So we all start a model, and we
tell it what we want it to do.

477
00:24:08,270 --> 00:24:11,890
And that was our way, without
obviously knowing about this

478
00:24:11,890 --> 00:24:15,400
yet, of doing the same to
a fairly complex system.

479
00:24:15,400 --> 00:24:18,990
We would tell this
agent to either do

480
00:24:18,990 --> 00:24:22,590
this sequence of buildings
and/or to construct these units

481
00:24:22,590 --> 00:24:26,910
and/or to behave like a
professional gamer or a novice

482
00:24:26,910 --> 00:24:27,810
in advance.

483
00:24:27,810 --> 00:24:29,510
That was our instruction.

484
00:24:29,510 --> 00:24:32,670
And then by doing
this, the agent

485
00:24:32,670 --> 00:24:37,670
was reconfigurable to play
in many different styles,

486
00:24:37,670 --> 00:24:41,790
controlled by the user,
prompted by the user.

487
00:24:41,790 --> 00:24:44,530
Now that's a similarity,
many differences.

488
00:24:44,530 --> 00:24:50,030
I mean, this agent was trained
on about 5 billion tokens.

489
00:24:50,030 --> 00:24:53,170
We know, of course, LLMs trained
on over 1 trillion tokens.

490
00:24:53,170 --> 00:24:56,890
So the order of magnitude
is quite different.

491
00:24:56,890 --> 00:25:00,810
These prompts were
structured, were again

492
00:25:00,810 --> 00:25:05,410
hand-tuned to the style
of vectors, binary vectors

493
00:25:05,410 --> 00:25:08,470
that we expected, rather than
expressed through language.

494
00:25:08,470 --> 00:25:11,610
It was not that we were
writing, hey, build an overlord

495
00:25:11,610 --> 00:25:14,335
and then build a [INAUDIBLE].

496
00:25:14,335 --> 00:25:17,170
These were binary
encoded, so to speak.

497
00:25:17,170 --> 00:25:20,830
And then these prompts,
unlike in today's LLMs,

498
00:25:20,830 --> 00:25:23,630
were derived by just
looking at the whole game.

499
00:25:23,630 --> 00:25:25,070
We could look at the future.

500
00:25:25,070 --> 00:25:26,450
We saw the whole game.

501
00:25:26,450 --> 00:25:28,850
We summarized it into
these vectors that

502
00:25:28,850 --> 00:25:34,230
were given from the first frame
of the game versus, of course,

503
00:25:34,230 --> 00:25:38,450
in current LLMs,
oftentimes, we have a prompt

504
00:25:38,450 --> 00:25:42,870
or an instruction that requires
human annotation in some form.

505
00:25:42,870 --> 00:25:46,230
Not always, but it's generally,
hey, there's coding competition.

506
00:25:46,230 --> 00:25:48,970
Someone comes up with a
nice coding competition

507
00:25:48,970 --> 00:25:51,230
problem with some instructions.

508
00:25:51,230 --> 00:25:55,040
And then we let the
agents try to solve

509
00:25:55,040 --> 00:25:57,660
for that particular coding
problem, for example,

510
00:25:57,660 --> 00:26:01,760
or a theorem proved
by LLMs, et cetera.

511
00:26:01,760 --> 00:26:06,360
So again, some similarities,
some differences here as well.

512
00:26:06,360 --> 00:26:10,280
But with these, we actually went
from that state of degeneracy

513
00:26:10,280 --> 00:26:15,080
of RL to actually being able
to start to see believable game

514
00:26:15,080 --> 00:26:17,080
states being achieved
by these agents

515
00:26:17,080 --> 00:26:19,300
that they didn't
experience the game,

516
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:22,960
but they imitated
the human knowledge,

517
00:26:22,960 --> 00:26:25,720
same as what we see
with LLMs before we

518
00:26:25,720 --> 00:26:28,520
do any post-training step.

519
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:33,120
Now on to, I think, a very
exciting area of progress,

520
00:26:33,120 --> 00:26:35,840
and I would say
probably not the most

521
00:26:35,840 --> 00:26:40,680
mature even in LLMs, despite
all the investment, which

522
00:26:40,680 --> 00:26:45,280
is post-training or using
reinforcement learning.

523
00:26:45,280 --> 00:26:48,000
So in StarCraft, we did a
few things, all of which,

524
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:51,620
actually, we also see
in some form or another,

525
00:26:51,620 --> 00:26:54,400
done for similar
reasons, to be honest,

526
00:26:54,400 --> 00:26:58,340
in current large
language models.

527
00:26:58,340 --> 00:27:03,580
So here is a fairly imperfect
way to measure Elo scores.

528
00:27:03,580 --> 00:27:07,120
You might be now fairly familiar
with Elo scores, thanks to LMCs,

529
00:27:07,120 --> 00:27:10,340
for example, which
scores, of course,

530
00:27:10,340 --> 00:27:14,020
your favorite LLM providers
against each other.

531
00:27:14,020 --> 00:27:16,420
But of course, I mean,
chess and many other games

532
00:27:16,420 --> 00:27:18,060
use Elo as a system.

533
00:27:18,060 --> 00:27:23,120
And think of things like 40 Elo
differences, not super large,

534
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:24,800
but it starts to be significant.

535
00:27:24,800 --> 00:27:27,900
400 is fairly
significant difference.

536
00:27:27,900 --> 00:27:32,640
So if we train from
scratch without human data,

537
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:34,780
we didn't get a
very strong agent.

538
00:27:34,780 --> 00:27:39,460
Just the supervised learning
policy was at 900 Elo.

539
00:27:39,460 --> 00:27:44,660
So that's a fairly
strong initial state

540
00:27:44,660 --> 00:27:48,650
to then kickstart the whole
reinforcement learning

541
00:27:48,650 --> 00:27:52,770
multi-agent system that we had.

542
00:27:52,770 --> 00:27:55,930
Now, the obvious thing
is that this policy,

543
00:27:55,930 --> 00:27:59,330
initialize it, and
then add, just use

544
00:27:59,330 --> 00:28:03,850
reinforcement learning vanilla,
and you get a bit of a boost.

545
00:28:03,850 --> 00:28:06,730
But what tends to
happen is, you might

546
00:28:06,730 --> 00:28:09,210
see some collapse of
behaviors, which also

547
00:28:09,210 --> 00:28:11,430
is quite commonly seen in LLMs.

548
00:28:11,430 --> 00:28:15,170
So the diversity of
the whole distribution

549
00:28:15,170 --> 00:28:17,770
that you've gathered
over imitation learning

550
00:28:17,770 --> 00:28:20,090
is lost, so to speak.

551
00:28:20,090 --> 00:28:23,070
So we added a
supervised KL penalty,

552
00:28:23,070 --> 00:28:27,010
which is a fancy way to
say, we distill the policy,

553
00:28:27,010 --> 00:28:29,170
the original
policy, onto the one

554
00:28:29,170 --> 00:28:31,490
we were training with
reinforcement learning.

555
00:28:31,490 --> 00:28:35,170
So in a way, you don't
overcommit too early

556
00:28:35,170 --> 00:28:37,110
to a certain particular
way of playing.

557
00:28:37,110 --> 00:28:41,730
You keep the diversity of styles
and strategies, et cetera,

558
00:28:41,730 --> 00:28:44,470
although that itself
was not easy to do.

559
00:28:44,470 --> 00:28:46,150
So we'll get back
to that in a second.

560
00:28:46,150 --> 00:28:49,930
But that helped quite a bit
keep things much better.

561
00:28:49,930 --> 00:28:52,750
So that boosted Elo by 400.

562
00:28:52,750 --> 00:28:54,150
And then the last
thing, which is

563
00:28:54,150 --> 00:28:57,430
this conditioning,
explicit conditioning,

564
00:28:57,430 --> 00:29:02,310
on the kinds of things we want
the agents to do, further boost

565
00:29:02,310 --> 00:29:06,310
the Elo score by
almost 150 or so.

566
00:29:06,310 --> 00:29:08,430
So all these aspects
came together.

567
00:29:08,430 --> 00:29:14,750
And we see obviously all these
being employed as well in LLMs.

568
00:29:14,750 --> 00:29:16,610
Another trick we
find, which was neat,

569
00:29:16,610 --> 00:29:20,870
is that for the value function,
which tells you whether you're

570
00:29:20,870 --> 00:29:24,870
winning or losing, even though
it's a partial observable game,

571
00:29:24,870 --> 00:29:28,570
we found that the value
function seeing the whole board,

572
00:29:28,570 --> 00:29:30,510
so to speak, was
much more accurate

573
00:29:30,510 --> 00:29:35,290
and helped reducing variance in
training, which was problematic

574
00:29:35,290 --> 00:29:37,410
and always using
reinforcement learning.

575
00:29:37,410 --> 00:29:40,470
So that's another trick we did,
which also, in different shapes

576
00:29:40,470 --> 00:29:44,350
and forms, is seen as
positive when you apply it

577
00:29:44,350 --> 00:29:49,580
to certain domains in current
large language models.

578
00:29:49,580 --> 00:29:54,620
So all these tricks and more
are used in modern LLMs.

579
00:29:54,620 --> 00:29:57,980
But what are the differences
between what we were doing here

580
00:29:57,980 --> 00:29:59,380
and LLMs?

581
00:29:59,380 --> 00:30:02,300
I mean, most
crucially, that rewards

582
00:30:02,300 --> 00:30:07,040
can't be fully specified
always, means StarCraft,

583
00:30:07,040 --> 00:30:08,820
you win, you lose.

584
00:30:08,820 --> 00:30:10,280
There are interesting things.

585
00:30:10,280 --> 00:30:14,160
StarCraft, actually, many
people resign before losing.

586
00:30:14,160 --> 00:30:16,340
So it's not that
all the data, you

587
00:30:16,340 --> 00:30:18,298
will get to the winning
condition necessarily.

588
00:30:18,298 --> 00:30:19,840
So there are some
fun problems there.

589
00:30:19,840 --> 00:30:25,740
But by and large, you can
compute success or not success.

590
00:30:25,740 --> 00:30:30,420
Whereas, of course in LLMs,
I mean, write me a nice poem,

591
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:33,980
it's just good luck, getting
humans to agree on that.

592
00:30:33,980 --> 00:30:38,300
So it's very, very tricky
to reward behaviors

593
00:30:38,300 --> 00:30:42,140
when you're going to much
more general problems.

594
00:30:42,140 --> 00:30:44,730
Now, that's kind of
annoying, definitely

595
00:30:44,730 --> 00:30:47,690
to me, which is, compute
envelope for LLMs

596
00:30:47,690 --> 00:30:50,690
are heavily skewed
towards pre-training.

597
00:30:50,690 --> 00:30:54,890
So you spend most of our
flops in pre-training

598
00:30:54,890 --> 00:30:56,790
and only a little
bit in post-training.

599
00:30:56,790 --> 00:30:58,950
And that feels wrong for
a variety of reasons.

600
00:30:58,950 --> 00:31:03,350
And certainly for AlphaStar,
it was not an exception.

601
00:31:03,350 --> 00:31:06,610
Most of the compute, most
of the weights updates

602
00:31:06,610 --> 00:31:12,250
were done in these experiential
or reinforcement learning stage.

603
00:31:12,250 --> 00:31:15,890
And then overfitting
is very common in LLMs

604
00:31:15,890 --> 00:31:19,290
because, it turns out, we do
not have an infinite amount

605
00:31:19,290 --> 00:31:22,290
of tasks that are
interesting for humans

606
00:31:22,290 --> 00:31:28,570
to solve or to perform that
would help train LLMs when they

607
00:31:28,570 --> 00:31:33,010
deploy it against real
humans or with real humans

608
00:31:33,010 --> 00:31:36,310
in the loop, the kinds
of things they would ask.

609
00:31:36,310 --> 00:31:38,190
There's a limited
amount of data.

610
00:31:38,190 --> 00:31:41,490
Whereas, for StarCraft,
every game is unique.

611
00:31:41,490 --> 00:31:43,970
And because it's constrained
by the environment,

612
00:31:43,970 --> 00:31:46,170
you don't see this
overfitting issue,

613
00:31:46,170 --> 00:31:47,430
which of course is related.

614
00:31:47,430 --> 00:31:51,510
Second and third bullet
points here are quite related.

615
00:31:51,510 --> 00:31:53,990
So let me take a sip of my tea.

616
00:31:53,990 --> 00:31:57,510
And let's get into slightly
more technical, more intuitive

617
00:31:57,510 --> 00:32:03,730
part of the talk about why do
we need multi-agent systems.

618
00:32:03,730 --> 00:32:07,630
What did we do in AlphaStar?

619
00:32:07,630 --> 00:32:10,550
So this was quite
fun at the time.

620
00:32:10,550 --> 00:32:15,470
Certainly, I enjoyed, of
course, the imitation learning.

621
00:32:15,470 --> 00:32:17,390
Sequence-to-sequence
was an example

622
00:32:17,390 --> 00:32:19,510
of imitation learning
very heavily.

623
00:32:19,510 --> 00:32:23,830
Reinforcement learning, I kind
of, hey, I know what's going on.

624
00:32:23,830 --> 00:32:26,150
As a scientist, you learn
you learn that domain

625
00:32:26,150 --> 00:32:28,190
as you join DeepMind.

626
00:32:28,190 --> 00:32:30,110
So it was kind of everywhere.

627
00:32:30,110 --> 00:32:33,050
Multi-agent, all that I
learned was during the project.

628
00:32:33,050 --> 00:32:36,630
And it was very fun and very
intuitive and very simple

629
00:32:36,630 --> 00:32:39,030
to see results, very drastic
results very quickly,

630
00:32:39,030 --> 00:32:43,660
just by trying, sometimes,
extremely simple ideas.

631
00:32:43,660 --> 00:32:47,100
So why did we want multi-agent
learning in AlphaStar,

632
00:32:47,100 --> 00:32:48,720
was to add robustness.

633
00:32:48,720 --> 00:32:50,660
So hopefully,
you'll get what that

634
00:32:50,660 --> 00:32:54,180
means through my explanations.

635
00:32:54,180 --> 00:32:57,160
So you have these models.

636
00:32:57,160 --> 00:32:58,500
They do amazing things.

637
00:32:58,500 --> 00:33:00,479
But we see them fail often.

638
00:33:00,479 --> 00:33:03,379
And that happened
certainly in AlphaStar.

639
00:33:03,379 --> 00:33:06,520
And we see this happen in
LLMs all the time as well.

640
00:33:06,520 --> 00:33:10,340
You ask simple questions,
like, is this number larger

641
00:33:10,340 --> 00:33:13,740
than this number, how
many Rs are in strawberry.

642
00:33:13,740 --> 00:33:18,660
And these models tend to
flop quite dramatically, even

643
00:33:18,660 --> 00:33:22,260
though they might be, I mean,
fuels [? medal ?] levels

644
00:33:22,260 --> 00:33:24,560
in some aspects of mathematics.

645
00:33:24,560 --> 00:33:27,080
They might fail miserably
in other aspects.

646
00:33:27,080 --> 00:33:34,540
So notionally, that is very
similar to the kinds of issues

647
00:33:34,540 --> 00:33:35,360
we encounter.

648
00:33:35,360 --> 00:33:39,400
We train a very good policy
with imitation learning

649
00:33:39,400 --> 00:33:40,860
and reinforcement learning.

650
00:33:40,860 --> 00:33:44,880
We then play the game as
humans, not in the self-play way

651
00:33:44,880 --> 00:33:47,880
that you keep getting
better Elo scores.

652
00:33:47,880 --> 00:33:54,120
And then we found that simple
strategies made the agent--

653
00:33:54,120 --> 00:33:55,560
it didn't react properly.

654
00:33:55,560 --> 00:33:57,320
And they were fairly simple.

655
00:33:57,320 --> 00:34:00,000
If you played at
the high level, it

656
00:34:00,000 --> 00:34:03,460
would really react to your
moves and feel super human.

657
00:34:03,460 --> 00:34:06,700
But it would not
understand some basics.

658
00:34:06,700 --> 00:34:09,760
So it very much
feels similar too.

659
00:34:09,760 --> 00:34:11,920
Although of course,
we're doing a lot

660
00:34:11,920 --> 00:34:16,800
of advancements in our
LLMs, but it felt similar.

661
00:34:16,800 --> 00:34:19,280
And using game
nomenclature, although you

662
00:34:19,280 --> 00:34:23,720
appreciate this is similar to
the failures you're seeing.

663
00:34:23,720 --> 00:34:27,800
We have different policies
or different examples

664
00:34:27,800 --> 00:34:34,000
that we could get
against our agents from--

665
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:37,790
some are very exploitative,
not that interesting,

666
00:34:37,790 --> 00:34:41,550
to actual strategies
that we would certainly

667
00:34:41,550 --> 00:34:44,570
encounter if we had this
agent online in the wild.

668
00:34:44,570 --> 00:34:47,550
So adversarial policies,
these would be,

669
00:34:47,550 --> 00:34:49,050
like, making the agent flip.

670
00:34:49,050 --> 00:34:51,030
It's almost like
adversarial examples.

671
00:34:51,030 --> 00:34:53,510
In neural nets, you change
a pixel a little bit,

672
00:34:53,510 --> 00:34:57,990
and a panda becomes an airplane,
according to a neural net.

673
00:34:57,990 --> 00:35:00,790
Similar things you can get
with a policy, where maybe you

674
00:35:00,790 --> 00:35:03,350
bring a unit, and you
move it in certain ways,

675
00:35:03,350 --> 00:35:06,750
and the neural network
just degenerates.

676
00:35:06,750 --> 00:35:10,370
So that's unlikely to be found
unless you let, of course,

677
00:35:10,370 --> 00:35:12,590
users probe it.

678
00:35:12,590 --> 00:35:15,790
But it's still the
most basic exploit

679
00:35:15,790 --> 00:35:18,990
you could think about from a
neural network perspective.

680
00:35:18,990 --> 00:35:23,870
Then we have exploiters that
are just repeatedly playing

681
00:35:23,870 --> 00:35:27,190
properly, so to speak,
against a single policy.

682
00:35:27,190 --> 00:35:29,630
But eventually, they
might find a weakness.

683
00:35:29,630 --> 00:35:31,750
And then they go deep
into that weakness,

684
00:35:31,750 --> 00:35:35,550
strategically speaking, up
until they just basically

685
00:35:35,550 --> 00:35:36,930
beat you all the time.

686
00:35:36,930 --> 00:35:41,030
And that's, in a way, similar.

687
00:35:41,030 --> 00:35:42,730
Both exploiter
and the next level

688
00:35:42,730 --> 00:35:46,570
are similar to what we're
seeing in LLMs these days.

689
00:35:46,570 --> 00:35:50,410
The third level is cheese,
which is a strategy that

690
00:35:50,410 --> 00:35:53,690
might generalize, might
be actually fairly robust

691
00:35:53,690 --> 00:35:56,890
against many different opponents
versus the second one, which

692
00:35:56,890 --> 00:35:59,730
is against just one opponent.

693
00:35:59,730 --> 00:36:02,410
But of course, it's not probably
the best optimal strategy

694
00:36:02,410 --> 00:36:05,290
for this game, although
mix and matching cheese

695
00:36:05,290 --> 00:36:09,610
and normal policies is commonly
done at the professional level

696
00:36:09,610 --> 00:36:11,730
of StarCraft as well
because you always

697
00:36:11,730 --> 00:36:14,170
need to be worried that
this strategy might

698
00:36:14,170 --> 00:36:15,102
occur at any time.

699
00:36:15,102 --> 00:36:16,810
You're not seeing the
board all the time.

700
00:36:16,810 --> 00:36:19,370
So to play the game
optimally, similar to,

701
00:36:19,370 --> 00:36:21,330
of course, things
like you see in poker,

702
00:36:21,330 --> 00:36:23,710
you always need to be
worried about many things.

703
00:36:23,710 --> 00:36:27,438
So you cannot go too far into
optimizing against an opponent

704
00:36:27,438 --> 00:36:29,730
that, even though you're not
seeing what they're doing,

705
00:36:29,730 --> 00:36:34,440
you don't know they're doing, so
unpredictability is important.

706
00:36:34,440 --> 00:36:36,840
So the third kind,
the cheeses, you

707
00:36:36,840 --> 00:36:39,520
usually see them at
even the highest level.

708
00:36:39,520 --> 00:36:43,360
And then there's normal
policies and optimal policies.

709
00:36:43,360 --> 00:36:46,480
Now, putting it
differently, this

710
00:36:46,480 --> 00:36:48,040
is called non-transitiveness.

711
00:36:48,040 --> 00:36:51,000
What this means is,
would this policy

712
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:54,380
work against a
different opponent?

713
00:36:54,380 --> 00:36:58,512
So the adversarial
thing, most likely not.

714
00:36:58,512 --> 00:37:02,040
The adversarial attack might
be specific to the weights

715
00:37:02,040 --> 00:37:03,680
of your neural network.

716
00:37:03,680 --> 00:37:07,880
Then the exploiter policy, you
train or you repeatedly play

717
00:37:07,880 --> 00:37:11,120
against the same opponent, who
cannot update their weights.

718
00:37:11,120 --> 00:37:14,280
So that one is specific
to one opponent.

719
00:37:14,280 --> 00:37:16,040
Cheese tends to generalize.

720
00:37:16,040 --> 00:37:19,280
So maybe when we're talking
about the R's in strawberry

721
00:37:19,280 --> 00:37:23,200
and so on, we see many models,
trained not very differently

722
00:37:23,200 --> 00:37:26,040
but somewhat differently
by different companies,

723
00:37:26,040 --> 00:37:27,340
have these weaknesses.

724
00:37:27,340 --> 00:37:30,280
So maybe, those
kind of exploits are

725
00:37:30,280 --> 00:37:32,120
more in this cheese
category, which

726
00:37:32,120 --> 00:37:35,420
are reasonably generalizable
but, of course,

727
00:37:35,420 --> 00:37:39,140
not very kind of
interesting or strong.

728
00:37:39,140 --> 00:37:43,240
I mean, no one, hopefully, just
uses these to cheese the model,

729
00:37:43,240 --> 00:37:44,080
so to speak.

730
00:37:44,080 --> 00:37:48,360
Then we have normal policies
and then the optimal policy,

731
00:37:48,360 --> 00:37:49,860
which, of course,
is very, very hard

732
00:37:49,860 --> 00:37:55,460
to find with deep reinforcement
learning and multi-agent.

733
00:37:55,460 --> 00:37:58,280
So if we put this
figure differently,

734
00:37:58,280 --> 00:38:01,140
one observation that
my colleague did

735
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:06,440
is that most interesting games
have these different axes.

736
00:38:06,440 --> 00:38:10,500
The z-axis here is the
strength, the inherent strength

737
00:38:10,500 --> 00:38:16,860
of the player, the Elo
score, or the z-score.

738
00:38:16,860 --> 00:38:20,660
But then within a
slice of this thing,

739
00:38:20,660 --> 00:38:24,500
you would actually
find different cycles

740
00:38:24,500 --> 00:38:27,980
of non-transitivity,
meaning that three models

741
00:38:27,980 --> 00:38:30,580
at the same Elo score,
especially if the Elo score is

742
00:38:30,580 --> 00:38:33,970
not very high, might beat
each other in a way that's

743
00:38:33,970 --> 00:38:35,170
non-transitive.

744
00:38:35,170 --> 00:38:37,070
Rock, paper, scissors
cycles, actually,

745
00:38:37,070 --> 00:38:41,970
that's a commonly used
abstraction that's very clear.

746
00:38:41,970 --> 00:38:44,450
Rock, paper, scissors,
one beats the other.

747
00:38:44,450 --> 00:38:49,010
So think of, we might have
three models, three agents,

748
00:38:49,010 --> 00:38:52,510
at the same level, but one
very strongly beats the next,

749
00:38:52,510 --> 00:38:56,030
and the next one very strongly
beats the next one, and so on.

750
00:38:56,030 --> 00:38:59,690
So there's these cycles
of non-transitive and many

751
00:38:59,690 --> 00:39:06,110
interesting games studied
here possess this property.

752
00:39:06,110 --> 00:39:08,830
And this is obviously
designed for zero-sum games.

753
00:39:08,830 --> 00:39:12,090
So for LLMs, you need to
make some abstractions

754
00:39:12,090 --> 00:39:13,830
to think about what that means.

755
00:39:13,830 --> 00:39:16,050
But we certainly
have Elo scores.

756
00:39:16,050 --> 00:39:18,890
And we certainly see
models having strengths

757
00:39:18,890 --> 00:39:22,850
that are not uniformly
the same across the same,

758
00:39:22,850 --> 00:39:25,630
let's say, level of LMC's
performance, for example.

759
00:39:25,630 --> 00:39:32,150
So that is an interesting aspect
of the complexity of the kind

760
00:39:32,150 --> 00:39:34,870
of task we're trying to solve.

761
00:39:34,870 --> 00:39:35,970
I'll skip this slide.

762
00:39:35,970 --> 00:39:38,210
Just there's the
reference from 2020.

763
00:39:38,210 --> 00:39:40,130
But that explains
this intuitively,

764
00:39:40,130 --> 00:39:43,390
this issue of,
there's an area where

765
00:39:43,390 --> 00:39:46,130
you're not so good, that
things are very non-transitive.

766
00:39:46,130 --> 00:39:48,270
And then as you become
very, very good,

767
00:39:48,270 --> 00:39:52,590
then there's a dominant
strategy that no one can beat.

768
00:39:52,590 --> 00:39:54,870
So that's how we're
seeing in most

769
00:39:54,870 --> 00:39:58,630
games, this kind of behavior.

770
00:39:58,630 --> 00:40:02,270
Now switching gears a
bit, how do you actually

771
00:40:02,270 --> 00:40:04,110
train these models?

772
00:40:04,110 --> 00:40:05,790
There's a few techniques.

773
00:40:05,790 --> 00:40:07,810
But all of them
have some issues,

774
00:40:07,810 --> 00:40:10,990
especially as the games
become strategically complex.

775
00:40:10,990 --> 00:40:13,970
So here is rock, paper,
scissors, actually.

776
00:40:13,970 --> 00:40:20,410
So each of the vertices here
are rock, paper, or scissors.

777
00:40:20,410 --> 00:40:22,630
A strategy is just
a distribution

778
00:40:22,630 --> 00:40:24,330
of what will you play.

779
00:40:24,330 --> 00:40:27,870
So if you play against an
opponent that always plays rock,

780
00:40:27,870 --> 00:40:29,760
well, that's a
very bad strategy.

781
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:33,220
And the optimal
strategy is uniform.

782
00:40:33,220 --> 00:40:37,960
So if you do self-play kind of
naively, and you try to say,

783
00:40:37,960 --> 00:40:41,380
I'm going to learn an agent that
plays rock, paper, scissors,

784
00:40:41,380 --> 00:40:46,100
you see this effect of
oscillating around the middle,

785
00:40:46,100 --> 00:40:49,620
and locally, you're very
strongly into one strategy.

786
00:40:49,620 --> 00:40:55,230
So sadly, that makes
convergence very hard.

787
00:40:55,230 --> 00:40:56,980
Now, what you can start
thinking about is,

788
00:40:56,980 --> 00:41:01,500
hey, let's add population,
populations of policies

789
00:41:01,500 --> 00:41:02,880
all playing against each other.

790
00:41:02,880 --> 00:41:06,120
So there's several
ways to do this.

791
00:41:06,120 --> 00:41:07,900
Here are the three figures.

792
00:41:07,900 --> 00:41:12,320
But these are starting to feel a
bit better, intuitively as well.

793
00:41:12,320 --> 00:41:12,820
if

794
00:41:12,820 --> 00:41:15,240
I want to learn proactively
rock, paper, scissors,

795
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:17,380
I'm exposed to many
players, maybe someone who

796
00:41:17,380 --> 00:41:20,700
has very suboptimal strategy
playing only scissors,

797
00:41:20,700 --> 00:41:24,860
but at least, I wouldn't
know that player a priori.

798
00:41:24,860 --> 00:41:27,400
So I might learn,
eventually, that I

799
00:41:27,400 --> 00:41:30,000
have to play the
optimal or the Nash

800
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:32,880
here, which is the middle
point, equal distribution

801
00:41:32,880 --> 00:41:34,720
of the symbols.

802
00:41:34,720 --> 00:41:37,840
Very simple game, though,
so obviously very toy.

803
00:41:37,840 --> 00:41:41,000
And different strategies
yield different results.

804
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:44,780
The one that is most hopeful,
but even in this toy example,

805
00:41:44,780 --> 00:41:48,360
it doesn't quite get always
the result that is desired,

806
00:41:48,360 --> 00:41:50,920
is the rightmost,
which is, you have

807
00:41:50,920 --> 00:41:54,280
a population of these agents
that you play against,

808
00:41:54,280 --> 00:41:59,280
and you play always against
the strongest opponent.

809
00:41:59,280 --> 00:42:03,440
That feels like a good
strategy to maybe set yourself,

810
00:42:03,440 --> 00:42:05,160
your whole system up.

811
00:42:05,160 --> 00:42:08,420
And we didn't quite
do this in StarCraft.

812
00:42:08,420 --> 00:42:10,080
But there's a lot
of these intuitions

813
00:42:10,080 --> 00:42:12,160
that played a big role.

814
00:42:12,160 --> 00:42:14,186
Let's see this played.

815
00:42:14,186 --> 00:42:15,060
We saw this.

816
00:42:15,060 --> 00:42:16,560
This is a real
example of what we

817
00:42:16,560 --> 00:42:19,360
saw as we trained
using self-play

818
00:42:19,360 --> 00:42:21,800
in AlphaStar in the early days.

819
00:42:21,800 --> 00:42:24,630
And let's use a
equivalent of rock, paper,

820
00:42:24,630 --> 00:42:28,350
scissors in StarCraft world,
which is three different kinds

821
00:42:28,350 --> 00:42:33,250
of units-- void ray, which
is an air unit; immortal,

822
00:42:33,250 --> 00:42:36,990
which is a very strong ground
unit that cannot attack the air;

823
00:42:36,990 --> 00:42:41,030
and then stalker, which is
kind of a unit that can attack

824
00:42:41,030 --> 00:42:44,510
the air but is not the
strongest ground unit.

825
00:42:44,510 --> 00:42:49,330
So void ray beats immortal,
immortal beats stalker,

826
00:42:49,330 --> 00:42:50,870
stalker beats void ray.

827
00:42:50,870 --> 00:42:53,610
And it's kind of true, if
you do it just naively,

828
00:42:53,610 --> 00:42:55,470
you just build lots
of these units.

829
00:42:55,470 --> 00:42:57,410
You most likely will see this.

830
00:42:57,410 --> 00:43:01,270
This actually represents the
game reasonably faithfully,

831
00:43:01,270 --> 00:43:03,550
so great.

832
00:43:03,550 --> 00:43:04,270
We set this.

833
00:43:04,270 --> 00:43:06,090
And we start from
imitation learning.

834
00:43:06,090 --> 00:43:07,430
So we have that players--

835
00:43:07,430 --> 00:43:10,390
I mean, there's a distribution
over different strategies

836
00:43:10,390 --> 00:43:14,550
that people will have done
when they play online.

837
00:43:14,550 --> 00:43:16,730
And our initial policy
is in the middle.

838
00:43:16,730 --> 00:43:19,570
It's not very good
at either of these.

839
00:43:19,570 --> 00:43:22,030
But it represents the
whole distribution.

840
00:43:22,030 --> 00:43:25,950
And as we trained, what
happened is, our policies,

841
00:43:25,950 --> 00:43:28,150
even with the distillation
losses and so on,

842
00:43:28,150 --> 00:43:32,950
started to only do void rays
really well, lots of them.

843
00:43:32,950 --> 00:43:36,630
Very good micromanagement, but
they became really, really,

844
00:43:36,630 --> 00:43:38,370
really specialized.

845
00:43:38,370 --> 00:43:40,690
So the whole population,
which was not really

846
00:43:40,690 --> 00:43:45,210
a population, this is just
pure self-play actually,

847
00:43:45,210 --> 00:43:50,650
the only strategy that became
present during training

848
00:43:50,650 --> 00:43:51,930
was void rays.

849
00:43:51,930 --> 00:43:54,890
And of course, that washed out
a little bit the prior knowledge

850
00:43:54,890 --> 00:43:57,650
that we came in, even
with distillation.

851
00:43:57,650 --> 00:44:00,230
And that meant that from
the agent's perspective,

852
00:44:00,230 --> 00:44:01,210
it was delusional.

853
00:44:01,210 --> 00:44:05,470
It was playing against itself,
void ray versus void ray,

854
00:44:05,470 --> 00:44:07,650
great battles,
awesome games, that we

855
00:44:07,650 --> 00:44:10,090
recorded during that time.

856
00:44:10,090 --> 00:44:13,670
If the enemy happened to
do immortals, no issue.

857
00:44:13,670 --> 00:44:16,210
These units dominate this.

858
00:44:16,210 --> 00:44:17,930
But if someone
just said, OK, I'm

859
00:44:17,930 --> 00:44:20,730
going to do these
other units, then

860
00:44:20,730 --> 00:44:23,220
these agents didn't even
encounter these anymore

861
00:44:23,220 --> 00:44:26,360
because all it saw
in its games were

862
00:44:26,360 --> 00:44:28,360
massive amounts of void rays.

863
00:44:28,360 --> 00:44:32,260
So sadly, this was not
a very robust strategy.

864
00:44:32,260 --> 00:44:34,460
I mean, if you force
a pro player to, hey,

865
00:44:34,460 --> 00:44:37,400
you have to do
void rays, only do

866
00:44:37,400 --> 00:44:40,160
that, no cheese, no
different strategies,

867
00:44:40,160 --> 00:44:41,820
then this agent was very good.

868
00:44:41,820 --> 00:44:44,680
But of course, people are
diversifying what they do.

869
00:44:44,680 --> 00:44:46,660
These are not the
only three strategies.

870
00:44:46,660 --> 00:44:49,040
There's early rushes,
all sorts of things

871
00:44:49,040 --> 00:44:50,760
that happen in these domains.

872
00:44:50,760 --> 00:44:54,200
So that really didn't work well.

873
00:44:54,200 --> 00:44:56,720
What we added, though, the
key notion in the league

874
00:44:56,720 --> 00:44:58,640
is to add exploiters.

875
00:44:58,640 --> 00:45:04,400
So as you train the model, the
main agent, this steal agent,

876
00:45:04,400 --> 00:45:06,840
it might still specialize
to the void rays.

877
00:45:06,840 --> 00:45:10,100
But then at some point
during the training process,

878
00:45:10,100 --> 00:45:11,880
like the reinforcement
learning, you're

879
00:45:11,880 --> 00:45:14,040
going to add a second agent.

880
00:45:14,040 --> 00:45:16,980
And this agent is going
to be an exploiter,

881
00:45:16,980 --> 00:45:19,360
meaning that it's
only going to care

882
00:45:19,360 --> 00:45:23,190
about beating this agent here.

883
00:45:23,190 --> 00:45:25,930
Its goal is going to
be, beat this agent.

884
00:45:25,930 --> 00:45:27,490
This agent cannot
train against this.

885
00:45:27,490 --> 00:45:28,810
So there's an asymmetry.

886
00:45:28,810 --> 00:45:31,690
The red gets to see the
games and learn from them.

887
00:45:31,690 --> 00:45:33,430
But the teal does not.

888
00:45:33,430 --> 00:45:35,790
So then what
happens naturally is

889
00:45:35,790 --> 00:45:38,650
that if the agent starts
to specialize to void rays,

890
00:45:38,650 --> 00:45:43,110
naturally, the exploiter
will start doing stalkers.

891
00:45:43,110 --> 00:45:47,710
So now, at least this agent
has seen that, hey, there

892
00:45:47,710 --> 00:45:50,190
are very good players
out there that will do

893
00:45:50,190 --> 00:45:52,070
this other unit that beats me.

894
00:45:52,070 --> 00:45:55,590
So it's going to be
exposed to this strategy

895
00:45:55,590 --> 00:46:01,230
and be able to react properly
and even say, run out

896
00:46:01,230 --> 00:46:04,870
of the optimum, which is
to build lots of void rays,

897
00:46:04,870 --> 00:46:07,790
to say, hey, maybe I actually
should also build immortals

898
00:46:07,790 --> 00:46:11,870
if I see that the enemy is doing
lots of stalkers, et cetera.

899
00:46:11,870 --> 00:46:13,490
So this is simplistic.

900
00:46:13,490 --> 00:46:16,270
But I think it gets the idea
of the algorithm we end up

901
00:46:16,270 --> 00:46:18,690
developing intuitively.

902
00:46:18,690 --> 00:46:22,090
In full, what we do
is this in steroids.

903
00:46:22,090 --> 00:46:25,930
So we had many
different agents that

904
00:46:25,930 --> 00:46:29,210
were created with the
sole goal of making

905
00:46:29,210 --> 00:46:32,490
the main agents, the agents we
actually deployed and became

906
00:46:32,490 --> 00:46:35,990
grandmaster against the
global population of players.

907
00:46:35,990 --> 00:46:40,870
So you can imagine we have these
three main agents, one per race.

908
00:46:40,870 --> 00:46:44,490
And then we have
different agents

909
00:46:44,490 --> 00:46:47,310
whose goal is just to make
the main agents stronger.

910
00:46:47,310 --> 00:46:50,370
So that's the whole
multi-agent system we designed.

911
00:46:50,370 --> 00:46:52,910
And the way it works is
actually super simple.

912
00:46:52,910 --> 00:46:55,170
We didn't experiment
a lot with this.

913
00:46:55,170 --> 00:46:57,650
So the main agents
are essentially

914
00:46:57,650 --> 00:46:59,650
playing against
themselves, playing

915
00:46:59,650 --> 00:47:01,610
against the other main
agents, and playing

916
00:47:01,610 --> 00:47:03,750
against the whole
population all the time.

917
00:47:03,750 --> 00:47:07,130
They're playing
against everyone.

918
00:47:07,130 --> 00:47:10,210
I'll get to how that
distribution of everyone

919
00:47:10,210 --> 00:47:11,850
evolves over time.

920
00:47:11,850 --> 00:47:16,270
But then these main exploiter
agents, they will basically say,

921
00:47:16,270 --> 00:47:20,560
hey, start from the prior,
try to beat, let's say,

922
00:47:20,560 --> 00:47:24,280
the three agents
maximally, so just cheat,

923
00:47:24,280 --> 00:47:26,740
cheat in the sense of,
I can train against you,

924
00:47:26,740 --> 00:47:28,600
you cannot train against me.

925
00:47:28,600 --> 00:47:31,880
Develop a policy that eventually
reaches a certain win rate.

926
00:47:31,880 --> 00:47:33,960
When the win rate is
very high, meaning

927
00:47:33,960 --> 00:47:39,320
I exploited you successfully,
only then you say, hey, here

928
00:47:39,320 --> 00:47:41,240
I am, I'm beating you.

929
00:47:41,240 --> 00:47:43,440
I'm a new player in this world.

930
00:47:43,440 --> 00:47:44,220
Deal with it.

931
00:47:44,220 --> 00:47:47,120
So this agent was
secretly training until it

932
00:47:47,120 --> 00:47:48,920
became good at exploiting.

933
00:47:48,920 --> 00:47:51,780
And when it became good,
it dropped a checkpoint.

934
00:47:51,780 --> 00:47:54,300
It showed itself
against the main agents,

935
00:47:54,300 --> 00:47:56,600
which now, all of a sudden,
could play against it.

936
00:47:56,600 --> 00:47:59,600
And it went, and it restarted
the same process again

937
00:47:59,600 --> 00:48:00,500
from the prior.

938
00:48:00,500 --> 00:48:05,240
So over time, you will
find different exploiters

939
00:48:05,240 --> 00:48:09,480
at different stages of training
against the main agent.

940
00:48:09,480 --> 00:48:11,940
And then we also added
league exploiters,

941
00:48:11,940 --> 00:48:13,540
which is the same concept.

942
00:48:13,540 --> 00:48:16,300
But instead of beating
only the main agents,

943
00:48:16,300 --> 00:48:19,460
these agents try to beat
everyone in the population

944
00:48:19,460 --> 00:48:23,080
by finding exploits that
work against everyone,

945
00:48:23,080 --> 00:48:28,040
rather than just the main agent,
which is a bit more like cheese.

946
00:48:28,040 --> 00:48:31,420
So we have main exploiters,
league exploiters, and then,

947
00:48:31,420 --> 00:48:33,340
of course, main agents.

948
00:48:33,340 --> 00:48:36,740
This was very fun to see, like,
the dynamics, the learning

949
00:48:36,740 --> 00:48:40,660
dynamics, very hard
to understand even

950
00:48:40,660 --> 00:48:42,680
in a game, what's
actually going on.

951
00:48:42,680 --> 00:48:43,960
I mean, intuitively, we know.

952
00:48:43,960 --> 00:48:47,940
But lots to do that are left on
the table from these AlphaStar

953
00:48:47,940 --> 00:48:49,740
league, as we call it.

954
00:48:49,740 --> 00:48:51,700
But it was very powerful.

955
00:48:51,700 --> 00:48:58,060
And then over time, we have over
1,000 agents that have different

956
00:48:58,060 --> 00:48:59,120
strengths.

957
00:48:59,120 --> 00:49:02,420
They will beat a previous agent.

958
00:49:02,420 --> 00:49:05,220
But eventually, the
main agents catch up

959
00:49:05,220 --> 00:49:06,720
and beat the exploiters.

960
00:49:06,720 --> 00:49:10,060
So there's all sorts of
interesting win-rate dynamics.

961
00:49:10,060 --> 00:49:14,008
And it can be represented in
this shape of the spinning top,

962
00:49:14,008 --> 00:49:14,550
a little bit.

963
00:49:14,550 --> 00:49:20,230
So the weakest agents here
are highly non-transitive.

964
00:49:20,230 --> 00:49:23,750
There are 30,000 RPS cycles.

965
00:49:23,750 --> 00:49:29,390
That means the cycle length
of non-transitivity is 30,000.

966
00:49:29,390 --> 00:49:33,330
So you can find 30,000 agents--

967
00:49:33,330 --> 00:49:35,250
I believe that's what
it means-- that beat

968
00:49:35,250 --> 00:49:37,850
the next one, the next one,
and so on and so forth.

969
00:49:37,850 --> 00:49:39,650
And as the agents
become stronger,

970
00:49:39,650 --> 00:49:41,250
they start to fully
dominate, which

971
00:49:41,250 --> 00:49:46,810
is also commonly seen, I mean,
in these interesting games.

972
00:49:46,810 --> 00:49:47,910
So that's an analysis.

973
00:49:47,910 --> 00:49:51,250
This is what happens in rock,
paper, scissors to give you

974
00:49:51,250 --> 00:49:55,290
an example, so much more
complex game dynamics.

975
00:49:55,290 --> 00:49:58,030
And then, as I said, it was
very fun to see this happen.

976
00:49:58,030 --> 00:50:00,970
So you would be training and,
all of a sudden, this red agent

977
00:50:00,970 --> 00:50:03,250
is an exploiter that
finds a cheese that's

978
00:50:03,250 --> 00:50:04,430
called cannon rush.

979
00:50:04,430 --> 00:50:10,050
So the blue agent,
that's the main agent,

980
00:50:10,050 --> 00:50:13,030
has not seen this strategy
enough so it can't really

981
00:50:13,030 --> 00:50:14,310
define very well.

982
00:50:14,310 --> 00:50:18,230
But eventually, by now playing
against this red agent that's

983
00:50:18,230 --> 00:50:21,910
now frozen in time and it will
just do the same strategy,

984
00:50:21,910 --> 00:50:26,210
eventually will be able to
defend and so on and so forth,

985
00:50:26,210 --> 00:50:28,470
so with workers, which
is the right reaction,

986
00:50:28,470 --> 00:50:32,430
and scout to find that you've
been, indeed, in this situation

987
00:50:32,430 --> 00:50:34,150
and so on and so forth.

988
00:50:34,150 --> 00:50:38,110
And we saw all sorts of
interesting dynamics as well.

989
00:50:38,110 --> 00:50:40,030
Anyways, there's
lots of cool games

990
00:50:40,030 --> 00:50:43,453
and, hopefully, analysis
that you can see online.

991
00:50:43,453 --> 00:50:45,370
I don't want to talk
about StarCraft too much.

992
00:50:45,370 --> 00:50:48,310
But it's a passion of
mine, as you can see.

993
00:50:48,310 --> 00:50:50,970
But yeah, this was
kind of very critical.

994
00:50:50,970 --> 00:50:52,950
And then in the
results, you could

995
00:50:52,950 --> 00:50:57,150
see that if you only train with
the population still, but only

996
00:50:57,150 --> 00:50:58,290
of main agents--

997
00:50:58,290 --> 00:51:01,030
so just have three agents
playing against each other

998
00:51:01,030 --> 00:51:03,090
and against themselves
in the past,

999
00:51:03,090 --> 00:51:05,630
that's called
fictitious self-play--

1000
00:51:05,630 --> 00:51:08,030
you achieve certain strength.

1001
00:51:08,030 --> 00:51:10,680
And this core is
not that perfect.

1002
00:51:10,680 --> 00:51:13,960
If you play as a human, you
would not be that strong.

1003
00:51:13,960 --> 00:51:17,740
I mean, these differences were
probably less than reality.

1004
00:51:17,740 --> 00:51:19,520
And we checked
these a little bit.

1005
00:51:19,520 --> 00:51:23,260
But it was hard to do these
experiments in the wild.

1006
00:51:23,260 --> 00:51:26,840
Then by adding main exploiters,
we became much stronger.

1007
00:51:26,840 --> 00:51:29,320
And by adding, on top of
that, league exploiters,

1008
00:51:29,320 --> 00:51:33,700
we had the final
system that was robust

1009
00:51:33,700 --> 00:51:36,420
against many different
strategies and, of course, very

1010
00:51:36,420 --> 00:51:39,740
strong at playing the game.

1011
00:51:39,740 --> 00:51:42,860
Now, a couple of details
and then I'll conclude,

1012
00:51:42,860 --> 00:51:46,240
is that the path of
learning matters.

1013
00:51:46,240 --> 00:51:49,345
And it's so hard to
know how it matters.

1014
00:51:49,345 --> 00:51:52,740
Basically, if you
are now an agent

1015
00:51:52,740 --> 00:51:56,900
and is exploited very often
or very rarely or initially is

1016
00:51:56,900 --> 00:51:59,720
never exploited, I mean,
all these changes, happily,

1017
00:51:59,720 --> 00:52:03,580
the policy you end up with
at the end of this process.

1018
00:52:03,580 --> 00:52:08,400
So there's lots of details
that are to be worked out,

1019
00:52:08,400 --> 00:52:09,910
even from a theory perspective.

1020
00:52:09,910 --> 00:52:13,520
But in these games, and with
deep reinforcement learning,

1021
00:52:13,520 --> 00:52:15,720
theory is also hard to come by.

1022
00:52:15,720 --> 00:52:19,420
And we had a couple of
strategies on how to matchmake.

1023
00:52:19,420 --> 00:52:20,740
So I'm the main agent.

1024
00:52:20,740 --> 00:52:23,200
There is literally 1,000 agents.

1025
00:52:23,200 --> 00:52:25,080
Which should I play against?

1026
00:52:25,080 --> 00:52:27,128
So we had two strategies.

1027
00:52:27,128 --> 00:52:28,920
One is, play against
the one that beats you

1028
00:52:28,920 --> 00:52:31,560
the most, which
is hard opponents.

1029
00:52:31,560 --> 00:52:34,720
And the other one is,
play against the one that

1030
00:52:34,720 --> 00:52:35,860
you're 50/50.

1031
00:52:35,860 --> 00:52:38,600
You're closest to
maximum entropy.

1032
00:52:38,600 --> 00:52:40,742
Because if the one
that beats you the most

1033
00:52:40,742 --> 00:52:42,200
beats you all the
time, there's not

1034
00:52:42,200 --> 00:52:43,880
going to be a learning signal.

1035
00:52:43,880 --> 00:52:47,360
So we tried this,
both strategies.

1036
00:52:47,360 --> 00:52:51,000
Variance was a little better,
like this one that maximizes,

1037
00:52:51,000 --> 00:52:54,600
kind of, that tries to get
to the sweet spot of 50/50.

1038
00:52:54,600 --> 00:52:58,735
But also, I mean, this is one
of the many parameters that

1039
00:52:58,735 --> 00:53:00,360
tells you that the
process in which you

1040
00:53:00,360 --> 00:53:03,840
create these main policies
matters a whole lot.

1041
00:53:03,840 --> 00:53:06,040
And then the other
part that was important

1042
00:53:06,040 --> 00:53:12,030
is that to generate interesting
counterstrategies or exploiters

1043
00:53:12,030 --> 00:53:16,350
or cheeses, having
controllability mattered.

1044
00:53:16,350 --> 00:53:19,390
So not only we started
from the prior distribution

1045
00:53:19,390 --> 00:53:21,110
that we had trained
in imitation learning

1046
00:53:21,110 --> 00:53:23,830
and say, hey, go and
exploit the main agent,

1047
00:53:23,830 --> 00:53:28,110
we also were opinionated about
how you should exploit it.

1048
00:53:28,110 --> 00:53:30,910
So we would sometimes,
not always, say,

1049
00:53:30,910 --> 00:53:32,850
I want you to exploit
the main agent,

1050
00:53:32,850 --> 00:53:36,190
but please do it
with this unit type.

1051
00:53:36,190 --> 00:53:38,710
Like, I mentioned how
we did this prompting.

1052
00:53:38,710 --> 00:53:42,370
So it was kind of tying your
hands behind a little bit,

1053
00:53:42,370 --> 00:53:44,250
saying, hey, I want
you to beat me,

1054
00:53:44,250 --> 00:53:46,430
but you have to use this unit.

1055
00:53:46,430 --> 00:53:50,030
And that added a component of
robustness and diversification

1056
00:53:50,030 --> 00:53:51,750
that also help.

1057
00:53:51,750 --> 00:53:54,830
So lots of different choices
here, not a lot of time

1058
00:53:54,830 --> 00:53:57,870
we had during the project
to fully study these.

1059
00:53:57,870 --> 00:54:00,750
But you can see lots of
different possibilities.

1060
00:54:00,750 --> 00:54:03,350
And it was very important
to have this diversity

1061
00:54:03,350 --> 00:54:04,730
in the main policy.

1062
00:54:04,730 --> 00:54:07,770
Of course, in LLMs,
that is trivial.

1063
00:54:07,770 --> 00:54:10,790
So they're very profitable.

1064
00:54:10,790 --> 00:54:12,150
There's a lot of diversity.

1065
00:54:12,150 --> 00:54:15,930
So there's not a problem
at all to think about, hey,

1066
00:54:15,930 --> 00:54:18,130
I don't know, solve
this theorem but do it

1067
00:54:18,130 --> 00:54:22,790
in Catalan or in Spanish or
do it in very bare bones.

1068
00:54:22,790 --> 00:54:25,970
All these things will add
some sort of robustness

1069
00:54:25,970 --> 00:54:30,450
to, maybe, new encounters
that you'll find,

1070
00:54:30,450 --> 00:54:34,570
meaning new prompts in LLMs
and so on and so forth.

1071
00:54:34,570 --> 00:54:38,810
So contrasting is very
exciting, multi-agent ideas

1072
00:54:38,810 --> 00:54:41,850
and space with LLMs.

1073
00:54:41,850 --> 00:54:45,850
I mean, one difference is
that users are, in general,

1074
00:54:45,850 --> 00:54:46,770
collaborative.

1075
00:54:46,770 --> 00:54:50,930
It's not a zero-sum
game that we play.

1076
00:54:50,930 --> 00:54:53,450
I go online, and I want
to achieve something, get

1077
00:54:53,450 --> 00:54:56,850
some information, help me with
some problem that I'm having.

1078
00:54:56,850 --> 00:55:03,010
So I'm actually collaborative
with the other agent, right?

1079
00:55:03,010 --> 00:55:05,040
So that's an
interesting difference.

1080
00:55:05,040 --> 00:55:08,920
That being said, we also
have adversarial users.

1081
00:55:08,920 --> 00:55:12,700
I mean, who doesn't like to
find weird failure modes?

1082
00:55:12,700 --> 00:55:15,480
And then Twitter
likes are, obviously,

1083
00:55:15,480 --> 00:55:19,880
one of the reward signals
that humans like to optimize.

1084
00:55:19,880 --> 00:55:25,200
And also more seriously,
eventually, these systems

1085
00:55:25,200 --> 00:55:28,480
become closer and closer
to AGI, very powerful.

1086
00:55:28,480 --> 00:55:30,440
So you might want to--

1087
00:55:30,440 --> 00:55:32,778
them for doing something
that they shouldn't be doing.

1088
00:55:32,778 --> 00:55:34,320
So there's going to
be adversariality

1089
00:55:34,320 --> 00:55:39,080
as well, trying to go
around how the system is

1090
00:55:39,080 --> 00:55:40,440
designed to be used.

1091
00:55:40,440 --> 00:55:44,430
So both aspects could use
multi-agent techniques,

1092
00:55:44,430 --> 00:55:46,680
such as the one I described
from the AlphaStar league,

1093
00:55:46,680 --> 00:55:48,440
differently.

1094
00:55:48,440 --> 00:55:51,920
Complexity is massive
compared to even a game

1095
00:55:51,920 --> 00:55:53,580
as complex as StarCraft.

1096
00:55:53,580 --> 00:55:55,140
So there's no clear reward.

1097
00:55:55,140 --> 00:55:57,480
We discussed this.

1098
00:55:57,480 --> 00:56:03,020
Prompting is much less clean
than bits of which units should

1099
00:56:03,020 --> 00:56:04,100
you build.

1100
00:56:04,100 --> 00:56:05,240
Prompting can be vast.

1101
00:56:05,240 --> 00:56:07,100
It's natural language.

1102
00:56:07,100 --> 00:56:08,040
It's unbounded.

1103
00:56:08,040 --> 00:56:11,100
It's very, very interesting,
the amount of complexity

1104
00:56:11,100 --> 00:56:13,020
we'll deal with here.

1105
00:56:13,020 --> 00:56:16,280
To the extent that even
these LLMs playing StarCraft,

1106
00:56:16,280 --> 00:56:18,140
which is a dream of mine--

1107
00:56:18,140 --> 00:56:19,280
They can play.

1108
00:56:19,280 --> 00:56:20,620
They're not very good--

1109
00:56:20,620 --> 00:56:22,320
is actually one of
the many domains.

1110
00:56:22,320 --> 00:56:25,380
So you could see a particular
case of current models

1111
00:56:25,380 --> 00:56:27,760
is that we might just
instruct them to, hey,

1112
00:56:27,760 --> 00:56:30,180
be a very good StarCraft
player, and then

1113
00:56:30,180 --> 00:56:32,020
give them control
of the environment

1114
00:56:32,020 --> 00:56:32,960
and see what they do.

1115
00:56:32,960 --> 00:56:35,140
And in fact, games,
more and more,

1116
00:56:35,140 --> 00:56:38,040
are starting to be
reused, turned around,

1117
00:56:38,040 --> 00:56:39,600
be used as an evaluation.

1118
00:56:39,600 --> 00:56:43,840
There's a Game Arena, I believe,
on Kaggle that is very popular,

1119
00:56:43,840 --> 00:56:45,020
for example.

1120
00:56:45,020 --> 00:56:47,740
And famously, I think
some of the LLMs

1121
00:56:47,740 --> 00:56:50,580
tried to compete Pokemon, et
cetera, so very cool domain

1122
00:56:50,580 --> 00:56:53,860
as well to test ideas.

1123
00:56:53,860 --> 00:56:56,780
I will say, some amount
of auto-red teaming,

1124
00:56:56,780 --> 00:57:00,870
et cetera, has been using
multi-agent techniques.

1125
00:57:00,870 --> 00:57:03,770
But I think, overall,
it's still in its infancy.

1126
00:57:03,770 --> 00:57:07,730
So there's lots and lots of
research to be done here.

1127
00:57:07,730 --> 00:57:11,410
So to conclude this sort of
past and future combination

1128
00:57:11,410 --> 00:57:15,930
and, of course, present
of LLMs, I would say,

1129
00:57:15,930 --> 00:57:18,550
there's lots pre-LLM agents.

1130
00:57:18,550 --> 00:57:22,850
There's lots of gems to learn
from from many different papers

1131
00:57:22,850 --> 00:57:24,770
and different problems.

1132
00:57:24,770 --> 00:57:26,210
Tool use was discussed.

1133
00:57:26,210 --> 00:57:30,430
Architectures, losses,
regularization, value functions,

1134
00:57:30,430 --> 00:57:33,950
I mean, lots of things we can
probably revisit very seriously,

1135
00:57:33,950 --> 00:57:36,050
and we should.

1136
00:57:36,050 --> 00:57:39,130
My strong belief is that
human priors are going

1137
00:57:39,130 --> 00:57:42,530
to be part of the path to AGI.

1138
00:57:42,530 --> 00:57:46,210
But we need to obviously also
build knowledge and experience

1139
00:57:46,210 --> 00:57:48,490
on top of that prior knowledge.

1140
00:57:48,490 --> 00:57:50,950
I've seen it succeed
enough times now,

1141
00:57:50,950 --> 00:57:55,490
including in AlphaGo, which
also use imitation plus RL that,

1142
00:57:55,490 --> 00:57:57,290
I believe, that's
certainly the shortest

1143
00:57:57,290 --> 00:58:04,710
path to AGI, although happy
to discuss that if useful.

1144
00:58:04,710 --> 00:58:09,830
Then in general, RL or
post-training on top of LLMs

1145
00:58:09,830 --> 00:58:12,630
still feels in their infancy
when I think and compare it

1146
00:58:12,630 --> 00:58:14,790
to a system like the
AlphaStar system.

1147
00:58:14,790 --> 00:58:19,190
We can train on thousands
of steps, maybe,

1148
00:58:19,190 --> 00:58:21,030
versus in this
AlphaStar league, we

1149
00:58:21,030 --> 00:58:25,070
use 10 to the 10 training steps
in post-training, so vastly

1150
00:58:25,070 --> 00:58:27,510
more compute used
in post-training

1151
00:58:27,510 --> 00:58:30,710
rather than in pre-training.

1152
00:58:30,710 --> 00:58:32,270
Of course, we also
have this issue

1153
00:58:32,270 --> 00:58:34,490
that the rewards are
fuzzily specified.

1154
00:58:34,490 --> 00:58:37,470
So that is definitely
a challenge.

1155
00:58:37,470 --> 00:58:41,270
And last but not least,
multi-agent RL, in whichever way

1156
00:58:41,270 --> 00:58:45,550
you can think of creating a
population of users and agents

1157
00:58:45,550 --> 00:58:48,230
talking to one another
and really optimize

1158
00:58:48,230 --> 00:58:51,050
and train for that, I
think that's very exciting.

1159
00:58:51,050 --> 00:58:53,870
And it's going to have,
probably, a new scalability

1160
00:58:53,870 --> 00:58:56,830
breakthrough, I can imagine,
in the next few years,

1161
00:58:56,830 --> 00:58:59,420
maybe from some of you.

